<section xml:id="sec-f-3-common-taylor-series">
  <title>Common Taylor series</title>

  <p>Taylor polynomials approximate functions near a chosen center.  Taylor series continue
that approximation indefinitely.</p>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-taylor-polynomial-remainder-table">
    <title>Taylor polynomial and remainder</title>

  <p>The Taylor polynomial of degree <m>N</m> for <m>f</m> centered at <m>a</m> is</p>

  <md>
T_N(x)
=
\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n.
</md>

  <p>That is,</p>

  <md>T_N(x)
=
f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2+\cdots+
\frac{f^{(N)}(a)}{N!}(x-a)^N.</md>

  <p>Taylor’s theorem with Lagrange remainder says that, under the usual differentiability
hypotheses,</p>

  <md>
f(x)=T_N(x)+R_N(x),
</md>

  <p>where</p>

  <md>
R_N(x)
=
\frac{f^{(N+1)}(\xi)}{(N+1)!}(x-a)^{N+1}
</md>

  <p>for some <m>\xi</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.</p>

  <p>Thus, if</p>

  <md>|f^{(N+1)}(t)|\le M</md>

  <p>between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, then</p>

  <md>
|R_N(x)|\le
\frac{M}{(N+1)!}|x-a|^{N+1}.
</md>

  <p>This is the formula that turns a Taylor polynomial into a controlled approximation.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-maclaurin-series-table">
    <title>Maclaurin series</title>

  <p>A Taylor series centered at <m>0</m> is called a Maclaurin series.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-3-common-taylor-series-0">
    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><strong>function</strong></cell>
        <cell><strong>series</strong></cell>
        <cell><strong>converges for</strong></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle e^x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n!}
=
1+x+\frac{x^2}{2!}+\frac{x^3}{3!}+\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell>all <m>x</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \sin x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^n\frac{x^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}
=
x-\frac{x^3}{3!}+\frac{x^5}{5!}-\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell>all <m>x</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \cos x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^n\frac{x^{2n}}{(2n)!}
=
1-\frac{x^2}{2!}+\frac{x^4}{4!}-\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell>all <m>x</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{1-x}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}x^n
=
1+x+x^2+x^3+\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>|x|&lt;1</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{1+x}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^n x^n
=
1-x+x^2-x^3+\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>|x|&lt;1</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \ln(1+x)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{n+1}\frac{x^n}{n}
=
x-\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^3}{3}-\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-1&lt;x\le1</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \ln(1-x)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
-\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n}
=
-x-\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{x^3}{3}-\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-1\le x&lt;1</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \arctan x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^n\frac{x^{2n+1}}{2n+1}
=
x-\frac{x^3}{3}+\frac{x^5}{5}-\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-1\le x\le1</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle (1+x)^p</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
1+px+\frac{p(p-1)}{2!}x^2+
\frac{p(p-1)(p-2)}{3!}x^3+\cdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>|x|&lt;1</m>, endpoints depend on <m>p</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <warning xml:id="warn-sec-f-3-common-taylor-series-12">
    <title>Radius warning</title>
      <p>Inside the radius of convergence, power series may be differentiated and integrated
term by term.  At endpoints, test separately.</p>
  </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-shifted-series-table">
    <title>Series shifted from zero</title>

  <p>A series centered at <m>a</m> is obtained by replacing <m>x</m> with <m>x-a</m>.</p>

  <p>For example,</p>

  <md>e^{x}
=
e^a e^{x-a}.</md>

  <p>So</p>

  <md>
e^x
=
e^a
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(x-a)^n}{n!}.
</md>

  <p>Also,</p>

  <md>\frac1{1-x}
=
\frac1{1-a-(x-a)}
=
\frac1{1-a}\cdot
\frac1{1-\frac{x-a}{1-a}},</md>

  <p>so, for</p>

  <md>\left|\frac{x-a}{1-a}\right|&lt;1,</md>

  <p>we have</p>

  <md>
\frac1{1-x}
=
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(x-a)^n}{(1-a)^{n+1}}.
</md>

  <p>Shifted series are useful when the function must be approximated near a point other
than <m>0</m>.</p>
  </subsection>

</section>
